<%# WHAT THIS CATEGORY'S MONEY IS (computed-claims spec §2, §3.4). Local: `presenter`
    (CategoryBudgetPresenter).

    ** EVERY FIGURE HERE IS A CLAIM NOW, AND NOT A HOLDING. ** The card read a `HoldingCalculator` —
    what had been ALLOCATED into the category, less what was spent out of it — and stood a
    `HoldingStatus` beside it in the row vocabulary. Nothing moves on the purpose side any more
    (§5), so there is nothing allocated to read: the category's money is a FUNCTION of its rules, the
    calendar, its spending and its dated adjustments, and the card is that function shown.

    THE COPY THAT HAD TO GO, AND WHY EACH SENTENCE WAS FALSE:

      "against what was available"                    there is no `available` — `free` is a
                                                        DEFINITION (`total − Σ claims`), not a pot
                                                        that spending is read against.
      "nothing here is ever swept back"               nothing is ever swept from anywhere; a rate
                                                        claim RESETS at the boundary by its own
                                                        definition (§3.1) and no money moves.
      "its money arrives when you distribute"         there is no distribute step (§1).
      "Move it back to available"                     there is nothing to move and nowhere to move
                                                        it to (§5).

    ** THE ROWS ARE §3.4'S, AND THEY ARE THE BUDGET PAGE'S OWN SENTENCES. ** `figure_words`,
    `when_words` and `claim_trouble_label` render the Budget page's rules table, Home's "This period"
    line and this card alike — one sentence about one rule across the three screens that show it:

      RATE      $310.00 of $400.00                    spent of this period's rate, over in red
      ACCRUING  $450.00 built up of $1,200.00          the running total against the target,
                next due Mar 1 · $200.00 per period      with the schedule beneath it

    A LINE PER RULE, because §3.4's sentences are per RULE and the card's own figure is their SUM: a
    category carrying a rate rule beside an item-backed bill cannot honestly print one figure, since
    the two are denominated in different things.

    TWO ARMS, AND THE LINE IS `Category#holder?`: a category with a `funded_since` is one whose
    rules accrue and whose spending counts against them. One without it claims nothing at all —
    not by a guard but by the formula, since its rules' walks have no day to open in. %>
<div class="bg-white shadow-sm rounded overflow-hidden border border-gray-100 p-6"
     data-holdings-card
     data-holdings-state="<%= presenter.state %>">
  <% if presenter.holding? %>
    <%# THE CATEGORY HAS A CLAIM. The heading names what it is — a TARGET, or an envelope — from the
        one reader, so the word and the bar below it cannot describe different things.

        ** "GOAL" IS RETIRED (rules-own-the-budget spec §7). ** A goal was a kind of CATEGORY, told
        apart by a figure on the category record; what tells these two apart now is whether a rule
        here is saving toward a DAY (two-shapes spec §2).

        ** AND THE WORD IS "Target", NOT "Fund" (fix wave — LOW-5). ** "Fund" is the noun of the
        retired model — money that had been MOVED into a pot — and every screen this plan rebuilt
        stopped using it: `EntryImpactPresenter#noun` says "bill" or "target", the trouble strip
        says "$X short", and nothing anywhere moves money into anything. What is true of both the
        bill's saving-up and the savings goal is that a figure is being reached by a day, which is
        what a target is. The reader keeps its own name (`#fund?`) — it is the shape, and renaming
        the predicate is a change to five files for one heading. %>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4">
      <%= presenter.fund? ? "Target" : "Envelope" %>
    </h2>

    <%# WHAT THE FIGURE BELOW IS MADE OF, SAID PLAINLY. It is the one thing about the computed model
        a user has to be told once: no money was put here, and none will be — the rules on the
        Budget page decide what this category claims, and the claim moves when a rule, an adjustment
        or the spending moves. The two arms differ only in what the money is FOR, which is what the
        heading above them already said. %>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
      <% if presenter.fund? %>
        This category is saving toward a figure by a day rather than resetting each period. Nothing
        was moved here to put it there — what it has is what its rules have built up since
        <%= presenter.funded_since.strftime("%b %-d, %Y") %>, less what has been spent here.
      <% else %>
        Nothing was moved here. What this category claims is worked out from its rules on the Budget
        page, the calendar, and what has been spent here since
        <%= presenter.funded_since.strftime("%b %-d, %Y") %>.
      <% end %>
    </p>

    <dl class="divide-y divide-gray-200 mb-4">
      <%# `Claimed` AND NOT `Holding`. The old noun was a fact about money sitting in the category;
          this is the same word the Budget page's group header prints over the same figure
          (`$400.00 claimed`), because it is the same sum over the same rules. %>
      <div class="py-3 flex justify-between gap-4">
        <dt class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-500">Claimed</dt>
        <dd class="text-sm text-gray-900 font-medium" data-figure="claim">
          <%= number_to_currency(presenter.claim) %>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <%# THE START DATE STAYS, AND THE LABEL NOW SAYS WHY IT MATTERS. It is
          `ClaimCalculator#accrual_start`'s first term — the day the accrual walk opens in — and the
          day `Entry.draining` starts attributing this category's spending to its rules. It is
          therefore the one date on the card that says which periods and which receipts the figure
          above is made of; anything earlier is in neither sum. "Holding since" named a fact about
          money that had been put here, which is the fact that stopped being true.

          THE WORD IS THE CATEGORY FORM'S FIELD LABEL, verbatim. That form is where this date is
          written and this card is where its consequence is read, and a date called two things on
          two screens is a user wondering whether they are two dates. %>
      <div class="py-3 flex justify-between gap-4">
        <dt class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-500">Claiming since</dt>
        <dd class="text-sm text-gray-900" data-figure="funded-since">
          <%= presenter.funded_since.strftime("%b %-d, %Y") %>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <%# THE PROGRESS BAR IS THE CAPPED FUND'S ALONE, and it measures the CLAIM against the rule's
        target. `progress_percentage` clamps at both ends, so a fund whose rules claim more than the
        figure they are aiming at draws a full bar rather than "130% complete".

        ** THE GATE IS `#bar?` AND NOT `#fund?` (spec §10.5; fix wave — MED-1). ** `Claimed` above
        is Σ EVERY rule's claim and the target is a ceiling on the FUND's built-up: beside a sibling
        bill they are different money, and `50% complete · Target: $2,400.00` measured one against
        the other. So the track needs the fund to be the category's ONLY rule. The heading and the
        Claimed figure are untouched — a sibling bill does not stop this category saving toward a
        day.

        (The other arm `#bar?` used to withhold was the UNCAPPED fund, which named no figure at all;
        that shape is retired with `budgets.carries-over` — two-shapes §7 — so a fund alone on its
        category always has a target now.) %>
    <% if presenter.bar? %>
      <% progress = presenter.progress_percentage %>
      <div class="mb-4" data-building-progress>
        <div class="h-2.5 bg-gray-200 rounded-full overflow-hidden">
          <div class="h-2.5 rounded-full <%= progress >= 100 ? "bg-status-success" : "bg-brand-dark" %>"
               style="width: <%= progress %>%;"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-between text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">
          <span><%= progress %>% complete</span>
          <span>Target: <%= number_to_currency(presenter.target) %></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# ** THE RULES, EACH AS §3.4'S ROW. ** The card used to list a rule's NAME and its STICKER
        amount — "$1,200.00" beside "Car Insurance" — which says what the rule declares and nothing
        about where it has got to, on the one card whose whole subject is where it has got to. The
        row says both halves now, in the Budget page's own words and off the same helpers, and the
        card still offers no editor: this is where a claim is read, /budget is where a rule is
        written. %>
    <% if presenter.rules? %>
      <div class="mb-4" data-holdings-rules>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-500 mb-2">
          <%= pluralize(presenter.rules.size, "rule") %>
        </h3>
        <ul class="divide-y divide-gray-100">
          <% presenter.lines.each do |line| %>
            <li class="py-2 text-sm" data-holdings-rule="<%= pool_rule_label(line.rule) %>">
              <%# STACKS BELOW `sm` for `budget_page/_rule_row`'s measured reason: a name, a figure
                  and a schedule are three things, and at 375px the figure wrapped into the name's
                  column. %>
              <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:justify-between sm:items-baseline gap-1 sm:gap-4">
                <span class="text-gray-700 truncate"><%= pool_rule_label(line.rule) %></span>
                <%# RED WHERE THE SPENDING HAS PASSED WHAT THE RULE HAD — `over?` off the
                    calculator, which reads the figure BEFORE the clamp at zero and is the only
                    reader that can tell "spent it exactly" from "spent more than there was". %>
                <span class="tabular-nums shrink-0 <%= line.over? ? "text-status-danger font-medium" : "text-gray-900 font-medium" %>"
                      data-rule-figure>
                  <%= figure_words(line) %>
                </span>
              </div>

              <%# THE CLAUSE UNDER THE ROW, WHERE IT EARNS ITS PLACE. Two kinds, and never both:
                  what is WRONG (over by $X, or an overdue date) takes the line in red, because that
                  is the news and it is the same string Home's strip prints about this rule; the
                  SCHEDULE otherwise, which is the half of §3.4's accruing row the figure has no room
                  for. A rate rule in good standing has neither and renders no element at all. %>
              <% if line.trouble? %>
                <p class="mt-1 text-status-danger font-medium" data-rule-trouble>
                  <%= claim_trouble_label(line) %>
                </p>
              <% elsif schedule = when_words(line) %>
                <p class="mt-1 text-gray-500" data-rule-schedule><%= schedule %></p>
              <% end %>
            </li>
          <% end %>
        </ul>
      </div>
    <% else %>
      <%# A HOLDER WITH NO RULE AT ALL. Every claim comes from a rule (§3.3), so a category with
          none claims nothing however much has been spent on it — and the $0.00 above needs that
          said, or it reads as an envelope somebody emptied. The old sentence named the two ways
          money used to arrive; there is one way now, and it is the button below. %>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4" data-figure="no-rules">
        No rule claims this category's money, so it claims nothing — its spending comes straight out
        of what's free to spend. A rule on the Budget page is what gives it a claim.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%= link_to budget_page_path, class: "w-full inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 rounded border border-gray-300 bg-white text-gray-700 text-sm font-medium hover:bg-gray-50 shadow-sm transition", data: { budget_link: true } do %>
      <%= heroicon "adjustments-horizontal", options: { class: "h-4 w-4" } %>
      <span>Rules on the Budget page</span>
    <% end %>
  <% else %>
    <%# THE CATEGORY CLAIMS NOTHING (§3.4's unbudgeted row). Not "it holds nothing" — that was a
        statement about a balance — but that no rule speaks for its spending, so every receipt here
        comes straight out of `free`, which the hero calls "Free to spend".

        ** THE `stranded?` ARM IS DELETED WITH THE MONEY IT WAS ABOUT. ** It printed "This category
        still holds $400.00, but it isn't set up to hold money" over allocations left in a category
        whose funding date had been cleared, and named the door back out. Nothing is ever moved into
        a category now (§5), so a cleared date leaves nothing behind: the state is unplantable rather
        than merely unreachable, which is why the arm is deleted rather than kept as a belt.

        THE SENTENCE BELOW IS ABOUT SPENDING AND IT IS EXACTLY TRUE OF THIS ARM.
        `CategoryLedger::ENTRY_CATEGORY_ID` yields NULL for a NULL `funded_since`, so no rule's lane
        can hold one of this category's receipts. (A rule written on such a category does go on
        accruing — `accrual_start` falls back to the rule's own birthday — and the Budget page's
        own band is where that is said. This card's subject is the category.)

        NO EDITOR, and the sentence says why rather than leaving a missing button to be noticed:
        nothing claims this money, and the way to change that is to give the category a rule on the
        Budget page — which is what the pointer below offers. %>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4">Unbudgeted</h2>

    <p class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-900 mb-1" data-figure="headline">
      Nothing claims this category's money.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
      Its spending comes straight out of what's free to spend. No rule reserves anything here, so
      nothing can run out — and nothing tells you when it has cost more than you meant it to.
    </p>

    <%# THE POINTER WHERE THERE IS ONE, AND THE STANDING OFFER WHERE THERE IS NOT — one route to
        /budget either way, never both. This arm IS the rate detector's population, so it is
        precisely the shape the panel is most likely to be proposing a rule for, and the pointer
        names what is waiting. Without a proposal the card would otherwise be a dead end. %>
    <% if presenter.suggested? %>
      <%= render "categories/_partials/show/suggestion_pointer", presenter: presenter %>
    <% else %>
      <%= link_to budget_page_path, class: "inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-medium text-brand hover:text-brand-dark" do %>
        <span>Give it a rule on the Budget page</span>
        <%= heroicon "arrow-right", options: { class: "h-4 w-4" } %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</div>
